    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nSixth-Generation Lexus LS Flagship Sedan Continues the Legacy, Albeit Only in CGI This Time Around\n11 photos\nPhoto: Theottle / YouTube\nYou wouldn’t say that based on the level of new model introductions from ultra-luxury brands like Mercedes-AMG, Ferrari, or Bentley, to name just a few, but it turns out that 2026 hasn’t been the greatest in terms of luxury demand.\nHowever, the likes of Mercedes- AMG with the GT 4-Door Coupe and the new GT SUV , Ferrari with the Luce, Bentley with the new Torcal, or the reinvented Jaguar with the Type 01, among others, continue to churn out one ultra-luxury high-performance model after another. So, why not Lexus too?\nThe Japanese automaker Toyota’s luxury division saw record sales back in 2025 when it delivered around 882k units globally, but its global and regional sales have taken a negative turn this year due to the perceived overall slowdown in global luxury demand. Also, it’s killing off the LS flagship sedan model in North America as it relies heavily on the crossover and SUV models, leaving only the refreshed IS and the all-new ES to keep the banner up across the passenger car sector.\nWell, there are even rumors that the big LS might bite the dust everywhere, not just North America – and some folks don’t like that, including a member of the imaginative guild of digital car content creators dwelling across the parallel universes of vehicular CGI. More precisely, this virtual artist, better known as \" Theottle \" on social media, says “the end of the Lexus LS marks the conclusion of an extraordinary 36-year legacy.”\nThe Lexus brand launched back in 1989 with the LS, as any fan of the brand will tell you, and for the author, “with production of Lexus' flagship luxury sedan reportedly ending after the 2026 model year and no direct sedan successor in sight, it's hard not to feel a sense of loss. To me, the LS is the single most important vehicle in Lexus history. It was the model that launched the brand and established its reputation for luxury, refinement, and engineering excellence. Simply put, without the LS, there would be no Lexus.”\nNo worries, as he’s got a solution; in his latest behind-the-scenes making-of video, he’s ready to continue the legacy beyond 2026. For starters, the author reminds us that the “LS name lives on through three concepts revealed in 2025: the LS Concept people carrier, LS Coupe Concept crossover, and LS Micro Concept box-on-wheels. Of the three, the LS Coupe Concept captured my imagination the most.”\nSo, he started thinking: “What if Lexus used the LS Coupe Concept as the foundation for an all-new flagship sedan? What would a modern interpretation of the LS look like?” This is the result after some CGI tampering with the sleek crossover SUV. Now it’s a not-so-classic four-door fastback sedan, a setup that is increasingly popular among automakers when it comes to ultra-luxury high-end models like the AMG GT 4-Door Coupe, Ferrari Luce, or the new Jaguar Type 01.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nLexus LS Lexus LS flagship sedan Lexus LS Coupe Concept CGI new generation rendering Theottle\nAbout the author: Aurel Niculescu\nAurel has aimed high all his life (literally, at 16 he was flying gliders all by himself) so in 2006 he switched careers and got hired as a writer at his favorite magazine. Since then, his work has been published both by print and online outlets, most recently right here, on autoevolution. Full profile\nUP NEXT McLaren Is Selling Five Formula 1 Cars Because It Wants Them Raced, Not Stored",
